# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF HERE, ON EVERY PLATFORM.
#
# Without this the CI job on `windows-latest` failed on its first step and
# reported every Go file in the repository as badly formatted:
#
#     these files are not gofmt clean:
#     cmd\atrium\main.go
#     internal\agent\agent.go
#     ...
#
# Nothing was wrong with any of them. A GitHub Windows runner checks out with
# `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every LF to CRLF on the way to disk, and
# `gofmt -l` correctly says a file full of CRLF is not what it would write. The
# blobs in git are LF, this machine is LF, and only the runner's copy was not.
#
# That is worth stating because the failure names the wrong thing. It reads as
# sixty unformatted files and it is one checkout setting, and the first instinct
# is to run `gofmt -w` over the tree, which changes nothing and fixes nothing.
#
# Set per extension rather than with a blanket `* text=auto`, because a blanket
# rule renormalises everything on the next checkout and one file here cannot
# survive that. See the bottom.

*.go    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mod   text eol=lf
*.sum   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one thing on this repository that is Windows-first, and the
# ecosystem's own convention is CRLF. Nothing here parses it with bash.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# NOT CONVERTED, AND NOT BECAUSE IT IS AN IMAGE.
#
# `toasts.js` carries two stray NUL bytes, which is why git already diffs it as
# binary and why a patch cut without `--text` silently drops it (filed as
# B2-51). A `text` rule would let git rewrite its line endings, and rewriting a
# file git cannot safely read as text is how the NULs become somebody's
# afternoon. `-text` says do not touch it either way.
internal/api/web/js/toasts.js -text

*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.exe   binary
